  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nEasterly flow between a high centered to our north and a low\npressure center to our south is helping to expanding low-level \nhumidity into our region, but actual conditions will remain dry. \nWe can't rule out a few light showers in southeast portions of our\narea, but we will generally stay on the drier western side of the\nlow pressure system as it lifts northward Thursday.\n\nThe pattern Friday into the weekend should at least see the \nprevailing influence of high pressure pull away from our region. A \nstrengthening southern jet stream will expand eastward from the \nPacific along the Gulf Coast, perhaps providing some ascent in the \nregion or at least helping to usher a shortwave trough across the \nInterstate 20/40 corridor. Confidence in measurable rainfall is low, \nbut can't rule out light showers at some point during the \nweekend.\n\nRounding out the forecast next Monday and Tuesday, not much can be \nsaid with certainty. The pattern is expected to remain progressive \ninto the middle of next week. Likely impact is for at least a \nreinforcing cold front early in the week. After trending warm with \nhighs in the 70s and lows in the 50s late this week and weekend (10 \ndegrees above normal), a reinforcing cold front would nudge us back \ntoward normal with mostly 60s/40s temperatures for the early week \ntime frame. /NF/EC/",
